{"problem": {"theorem": "Define $f_{n}:[0,1] \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ by the equation $f_{n}(x)=x^{n}$. Show that the sequence $\left(f_{n}\right)$ does not converge uniformly.", "assumptions": [], "domain": "topology", "explicit_subgoals": []}, "target": {"node_id": "n2", "version": 1}, "target_node": {"text": "The sequence $\left(f_n\right)_n$ does not converge uniformly, since given $0<\varepsilon<1$ and $N \in \mathbb{Z}_{+}$, for $x=\varepsilon^{1 / N}$ we have $d\left(f_N(x), f(x)\right)=\varepsilon$.", "claim": "Claims nonuniform convergence by choosing, for each positive integer $N$ and $0<\varepsilon<1$, the point $x=\varepsilon^{1/N}$. The calculation requires the unstated pointwise limit $f(x)=0$ for $x<1$; fixing one such $\varepsilon$, for example $1/2$, would then match the negation of uniform convergence.", "scope": [], "depends_on": []}, "closed_predecessors": [], "patch": {"base_state_digest": "81e17daa5d6964edfa4f1f733447e8e0a5ed8ad8338736ce6a0d6a5646c7b917", "certificate_id": "3505c990c3ce2692989bd4759c1ac02362457a7ee76f45dc63e2e390e9c87073", "target": {"node_id": "n2", "version": 1}, "operation": "insert_before", "nodes": [{"node_id": "n2_bridge_limit", "text": "For every $x\in[0,1)$, $x^n\to0$, while $f_n(1)=1$ for every $n$. Thus $(f_n)$ converges pointwise to the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=0$ for $x<1$ and $f(1)=1$. Any uniform limit would also be a pointwise limit, so it would necessarily equal this $f$.", "claim": "Establishes the pointwise limit and shows that any possible uniform limit must be this function.", "depends_on": [], "scope": [], "goal_refs": []}], "target_dependencies_after": ["n2_bridge_limit"], "counterexample": null, "reason": "This bridge defines and justifies the function used in the target. Since $0<\varepsilon<1$ implies $x=\varepsilon^{1/N}<1$, the target then has $f(x)=0$ and $|f_N(x)-f(x)|=\varepsilon$, contradicting the uniform convergence criterion for every $N$."}, "patch_digest": "8321dac32ef264f45c5103db78f7b8f30e7953268659b80a4318ef44474750e1", "certificate": {"certificate_id": "3505c990c3ce2692989bd4759c1ac02362457a7ee76f45dc63e2e390e9c87073", "problem_digest": "e5fcea369deb30b6c131db77aba1a1b879c1171eb489f4427aebc25df4006bda", "target": {"node_id": "n2", "version": 1}, "graph_digest": "1dfa4bf8d15529f66a4ffbb2f1bb3884aba7bf42aaa2f5dd7ccf128d4f1ac463", "confirmation": "confirmed", "kind": "gap", "failed_edge": "No predecessor establishes the function f or the pointwise limit of (f_n), yet the conclusion uses f(x) and its value at x=ε^(1/N).", "quote": "The sequence $\left(f_n\right)_n$ does not converge uniformly, since given $0<\varepsilon<1$ and $N \in \mathbb{Z}_{+}$, for $x=\varepsilon^{1 / N}$ we have $d\left(f_N(x), f(x)\right)=\varepsilon$.", "reason": "The equality is unsupported because $f$ is undefined. One must first establish the pointwise limit $f(x)=0$ for $x<1$ and $f(1)=1$. Then, for any fixed $\varepsilon_0\in(0,1)$ and every $N$, choosing $n=N$ and $x=\varepsilon_0^{1/N}<1$ gives $|f_N(x)-f(x)|=\varepsilon_0$, which negates uniform convergence. The wording “given ε” can supply such a fixed ε, so the essential gap is the missing definition and justification of $f$."}, "proposed_proof": "\begin{proof}
For every $x\in[0,1)$, $x^n\to0$, while $f_n(1)=1$ for every $n$. Thus $(f_n)$ converges pointwise to the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=0$ for $x<1$ and $f(1)=1$. Any uniform limit would also be a pointwise limit, so it would necessarily equal this $f$.
The sequence $\left(f_n\right)_n$ does not converge uniformly, since given $0<\varepsilon<1$ and $N \in \mathbb{Z}_{+}$, for $x=\varepsilon^{1 / N}$ we have $d\left(f_N(x), f(x)\right)=\varepsilon$.
We can also apply Theorem 21.6: the convergence is not uniform since $f$ is not continuous.
\end{proof}"}

